# Provider Module Capabilities

This file documents the expected functionality baseline for every provider module.
Each provider must implement the common capabilities below, then may extend them
with provider-specific features where technically required.

---

## Common Capabilities Baseline

Every provider module MUST implement all of the following:

### SSH & Secrets
- Generate RSA 4096-bit SSH key pair
- Write keys to `env/<PROVIDER>/<workspace>/`

### Compute Topology
- N masters and N workers, independently configurable
- Separate `masters` and `workers` objects in the variable schema
- Per-role compute sizing:
  - CPU / RAM / disk size
  - Extra disks (structured): `size_gb`, `mount_path`, `filesystem`, `label`
- Scale up/down: adding or removing VMs

### Cloud-Init
- Selectable cloud-init variant via `cluster.cloud_init_selected`:
  - `default`, `k3s`, `rke2`
- Cloud-init source paths MUST reference `providers/shared/cloud-init/$type/`
- Inject username + SSH public key
- Optional OS upgrade control via `cluster.package_upgrade_enabled`

### Optional Cloud-Init Inputs
- `extra_packages` (list of strings)
- `k3s` block: `version`, `token`, `tls_sans`, `etcd_enabled`, `traefik_enabled`,
  `servicelb_enabled`, `local_storage_enabled`, `metrics_server_enabled`, `flannel_enabled`
- `rke2` block: `version`, `token`, `tls_sans`, `etcd_enabled`,
  `ingress_nginx_enabled`, `metrics_server_enabled`
- Cluster token auto-generation (writes `.token` file)
- `ansible.pull` block: `repo`, `branch`, `playbook`, `token`, `timer`

### Ansible Integration
- Inventory output compatible with `providers/shared/inventory/hosts.tpl`
- Generate `ansible.cfg` with remote user, inventory path, private key
- Post-deployment playbooks (shared):
  - Cloud-init readiness check
  - TLS SAN reconciliation (k3s/rke2)
  - Kubeconfig fetch with endpoint rewrite (k3s/rke2)

### Outputs
- `cluster_nodes` — structured list of all provisioned nodes
- `kubeconfig_command` — command to retrieve the kubeconfig
- `kube_api_endpoint` — public-facing API address

### Cluster Identity
- `cluster.id` for resource naming
- `cluster.domain` for DNS naming
- `cluster.node_name_format`: `"serial"` (shared sequence) or `"role"` (per-role sequence)

---

## Provider-Specific Capabilities

### Azure

| Capability | Details |
|---|---|
| **Credentials** | `azure_subscription_id`, `azure_client_id`, `azure_client_secret`, `azure_tenant_id` |
| **Region** | `cluster.region` (e.g. `westeurope`) |
| **DNS** | Azure Private DNS Zone + A records, linked to VNet |
| **Public IPs** | Every VM gets a `Standard` SKU static public IP |
| **Private IP** | Dynamic allocation (Azure-managed) |
| **NSG rules** | Configurable via `nsg_rules` map (port, name, description, source_address). Defaults for SSH (22) and K8s API (6443). Source address auto-populated from `ifconfig.me` when empty. |
| **Subnet** | Derived from `network.cidr` via `cidrsubnet()` |
| **Hostname prefix** | From `os_catalog.hostname_prefix` (e.g. `slaz`) |
| **Extra disks** | Uses Azure LUN numbering |
| **TLS SANs** | Public IP embedded at install time |
| **Networking** | Fully ARM-managed (no cloud-init network config) |
| **Cloud-init variables** | `current_private_ip`, `current_public_ip` (both known at plan time) |
| **Ansible inventory** | Public IP based |

### Libvirt

| Capability | Details |
|---|---|
| **Connection** | Local `qemu:///system` by default, remote `qemu+ssh://` via `libvirt` config block |
| **Network mode** | `nat`, `route`, or `bridge` — validated with mode-specific requirements |
| **IP type** | `dhcp` or `static` |
| **Per-role IP/MAC** | Optional `ip_addresses` and `mac_addresses` lists (unique to Libvirt) |
| **Per-role CPU / RAM** | Defined directly on `infra.masters` / `infra.workers` (`cpu`, `memory_gb`) |
| **Gateway** | Optional, auto-derived from CIDR for nat/route, required for bridge+static |
| **DNS servers** | `network.extra_dns` (default `["8.8.8.8", "8.8.4.4"]`); libvirt gateway prepended in nat/route |
| **Storage pool** | `cluster.factory_root_path` (default `/srv`) |
| **Cloud-init delivery** | Via `libvirt_cloudinit_disk` ISO |
| **Network config** | `network_config.cfg.tftpl` — DHCP and static IP support + DNS |
| **Artifact mode** | `gitops_artifacts_mode` in `{"manual", "gitops"}` |
| **CPU mode** | `host-passthrough` |
| **Ansible inventory** | Operator endpoint (coalesces static IP, runtime IP, FQDN) |

### OVH

| Capability | Details |
|---|---|
| **Credentials** | `ovh_endpoint`, `ovh_application_key`, `ovh_application_secret`, `ovh_consumer_key`, `ovh_project_service_name` |
| **Region** | `cluster.region` (e.g. `GRA9`) |
| **Private network** | `network.private.cidr` (required) + `network.private.vlan_id` (optional, default `0`). Creates `ovh_cloud_project_network_private` + subnet. VLAN ID allows multiple clusters in the same region (valid range: 0–4000). |
| **Deterministic private IPs** | Computed from `cidrhost(local.private_cidr, offset)` |
| **Dual NICs** | Public (`ens3`) + private (`ens4`). Private NIC netplan override written via cloud-init to prevent competing default routes. |
| **Gateway IP (subnet)** | `enable_gateway_ip` set to `local.lb_enabled` — only reserves a gateway IP on the subnet when a load balancer is present. Eliminates unnecessary DHCP default route on `ens4` when no LB is needed. |
| **Load balancer** | `network.kube_api.load_balancer` — creates OVH Public Cloud Load Balancer (Octavia-based) with: |
| | - `enabled` (bool, default `false`) |
| | - `flavor` (string: `small`, `medium`, `large`, `xl`) |
| | - `gateway_model` (string: `s`, `m`, `l`, `xl`, `2xl` — default `s`) |
| | - TCP listener on port 6443 with health monitor |
| | - Backend pool members: all master private IPs on port 6443 |
| | - Floating IP created via `floating_ip_create` |
| | - Gateway created via `gateway_create` |
| **API endpoint** | `network.kube_api.endpoint` — resolution priority: LB floating IP > literal value > DNS name > first master public IP > first master private IP |
| **Image discovery** | Search patterns matching image names in region (filters nvidia, prefers non-UEFI) |
| **Flavor discovery** | Flavor IDs looked up by name from `data.ovh_cloud_project_flavors` |
| **Public IP validation** | Validates all VMs received public IPv4 addresses |
| **Gateway cleanup** | `cleanup_gateway.py` — removes LB gateway + orphan floating IP on destroy (prevents subnet deletion blocking) |
| **Ansible inventory** | Public IP based |
| **Deploy-time checks** | `checks.tf`: multi-master requires private CIDR, LB requires private CIDR, LB flavor exists, CIDR provides enough IPs |

---

## Current Limitations

### Azure
- No `node_name_format` option (always role-based with `os_catalog.hostname_prefix`)
- No static IP option (`ip_type` always `"dhcp"`)
- No gitops artifact mode (always writes local files)
- No OVH-style load balancer (uses Azure-native constructs)

### Libvirt
- No `region` concept (local hypervisor)
- No public IP addresses (no OVH/Azure-style public IP model)

### OVH
- Extra disks: blocked by validation (must be empty)
- Custom root disk sizing: blocked at 40 GB
- Inventory remains public-IP based even when private network exists (private inventory not yet implemented)
- Multi-master + workers scenario (3m/2w) not yet in the test matrix
- Provider version not pinned in `providers.tf`
- `required_version` constraint commented out in `providers.tf`

---

## Test Matrix

| Provider | Infra | k8s type | Config |
|---|---|---|---|
| AZ | 1 master | k3s | default |
| AZ | 3 masters / 2 workers | k3s | default |
| AZ | 1 master | rke2 | default |
| AZ | 3 masters / 2 workers | rke2 | default |
| KVM | 1 master | k3s | default |
| KVM | 3 masters / 2 workers | k3s | default |
| KVM | 1 master | rke2 | default |
| KVM | 3 masters / 2 workers | rke2 | default |
| OVH | 1 master | k3s | default |
| OVH | 1 master / 2 workers | k3s | default |
| OVH | 3 masters | k3s | default |
| OVH | 1 master | rke2 | default |
| OVH | 1 master / 2 workers | rke2 | default |
| OVH | 3 masters | rke2 | default |

All scenarios validated with both `k3s` and `rke2` cloud-init modes where supported.
Generated inventory and kubeconfig artifacts expected under `env/<PROVIDER>/<workspace>/`.

### Validation expectations

At minimum, each provider change should preserve:

- Single-master k3s flow
- HA-style multi-master + workers flow
- Both `k3s` and `rke2` cloud-init modes
- Generated inventory and kubeconfig artifacts
